\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1  (id)\b contentView\b0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns the NSBox\'27s content view. The content view is created automatically when the box is created, and resized as the box is resized (you should never send frame-altering messages directly to a box\'27s content view). You can replace it with an NSView of your own through the \b setContentView:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker8612 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSBox-contentViewMargins];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 contentViewMargins\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1  (NSSize)\b contentViewMargins\b0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns the distances between the border and the content view. By default, on Mach systems both the width (the horizontal distance between the innermost edge of the border and the content view) and the height (the vertical distance between the innermost edge of the border and the content view) of the returned NSSize are 5.0 in the box\'27s coordinate system.\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker9212 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSBox-setBorderType:];}
