\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker11213 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSBox-setContentViewMargins:];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 setContentViewMargins:\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1  (void)\b setContentViewMargins:\b0 (NSSize)\i offsetSize\i0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Sets the horizontal and vertical distance between the border of the NSBox and its content view. The \i horizontal\i0  value is applied (reckoned in the box\'27s coordinate system) fully and equally to the left and right sides of the box. The \i vertical\i0  value is similarly applied to the top and bottom.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Unlike changing a box\'27s other attributes, such as its title position or border type, changing the offsets \i doesn\'27t\i0  automatically resize the content view. In general, you should send a \b sizeToFit\b0  message to the box after changing the size of its offsets. This causes the content view to remain unchanged while the box is sized to fit around it.\
\fs14\
