\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1  (void)\b sizeToFit\b0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Resizes and moves the NSBox\'27s content view so that it just encloses its subviews. The box itself is then moved and resized to wrap around the content view. The box\'27s width is constrained so its title will be fully displayed. \
\fs14\
\fs28 You should invoke this method after:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab Adding a subview (to the content view).\
\'b7\tab Altering the size or location of such a subview.\
\'b7\tab Setting the margins around the content view.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The mechanism by which the content view is moved and resized depends on whether the object responds to its own \b sizeToFit\b0  message: If it does respond, then that message is sent and the content view is expected to be so modified. If the content view doesn\'27t respond, the box moves and resizes the content view itself. \
\fs14\
